BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2419@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T150000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T153000
SUMMARY:Reducing kernel stack memory (on arm64)
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Kernel Memory Management MC · Will Deacon · https://lpc.event
 s/event/20/contributions/2419/\n\nThere have been a bunch of attempts [1] 
 to reduce the memory consumed by kernel stacks for x86 and arm64\, largely
  based around the idea of dynamically growing the kernel stack allocation 
 based on page faults. This poses what appear to be insurmountable challeng
 es\, as it introduces complexity into the architecture exception entry cod
 e (which needs to be able to transition cleanly to a new stack) but also m
 eans that the kernel must be able to allocate memory from any context at a
 ll.\n\nI would like to discuss and explore alternatives to dynamic stack a
 llocation based on some initial rework I have started of the arm64 excepti
 on entry code[2]. Specifically:\n\n- Revisiting the decision to move from 
 8k to 16k stacks\n- Changing the kernel stack size at boot time\n- Changin
 g the kernel stack size per task\n- Controlling the kernel stack size from
  userspace\n\nI think it would also be useful to talk briefly about the st
 icking points with dynamic kernel stacks and whether there is anything tha
 t can be carried forward from that work.\n\nMy motivation is based on Andr
 oid vendors having various out-of-tree implementations of the dynamic kern
 el stack series and I would like to see if there are alternatives that we 
 can provide upstream so that we can all stop carrying the broken stuff aro
 und in our pockets.\n\n[1] https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260424191456.2679
 717-1-stevensd@google.com/\n[2] https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kerne
 l/git/will/linux.git/log/?h=overflow-stack
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2419/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2421@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T153000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T155000
SUMMARY:Better Anon (Swap) Readahead
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Kernel Memory Management MC · Kairui Song · https://lpc.event
 s/event/20/contributions/2421/\n\nWhen handling swap page faults\, the ker
 nel often don't reads just a single page. For legacy HDDs\, an entire clus
 ter is read in. For SSDs\, we check the VMA and page tables to see if near
 by pages are likely to be needed soon\, using a basic statistic-driven heu
 ristic. For compressed RAM (like zram\, and potentially soon zswap)\, trad
 itional readahead is skipped entirely. Meanwhile\, enabling (m)THP swap-in
  currently acts as kind of a speculative fetching.\n\nThe current implemen
 tation is not really ideal. For example\, why can't we extend (m)THP suppo
 rt to SSDs by merging readahead detection with (m)THP lookaround? Why can'
 t we enable a lightweight readahead for compressed RAM as well? We also ne
 ed a smarter heuristic\, where workingset shadow entries could provide a m
 uch better hint than basic per-VMA statistics?\n\nWith so much active deve
 lopment in the swap subsystem\, the goal of this session is to consolidate
  these scattered paths\, and explore whether the swap readahead mechanism 
 can share common\, useful infrastructure.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2421/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2416@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T155000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T161000
SUMMARY:DMA-buf cgroup accounting for proxy allocators: attributing buffers
  to the right consumer
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Kernel Memory Management MC · Albert Esteve\, T.J. Mercier · 
 https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2416/\n\nOn embedded and automot
 ive Linux systems\, a single daemon often allocates DMA-buf memory on beha
 lf of other clients. Today\, most of that memory is invisible to cgroup ac
 counting\, only system-heap buffers can carry a charge via __GFP_ACCOUNT\,
  and even then it lands on the allocator’s cgroup rather than the app th
 at requested the buffer.\n\nThe misattribution has real consequences on an
 y system that uses cgroup memory limits to drive reclaim or enforce resour
 ce budgets\, as untracked buffers silently skew pressure signals. For exam
 ple\, on Android\, where app memory limits depend on per-app accounting\; 
 or on automotive systems\, where strict Freedom From Interference (FFI) is
  a key requirement and a central allocator absorbing the charge of its cli
 ents across domains breaks any meaningful resource isolation argument.\n\n
 In this session we will discuss the design space for fixing the proxy-allo
 cator problem\, presenting the context from our proposal in upstream. Shou
 ld the charge be attributed at allocation time\, or before export time? Sh
 ould the allocator identify the target via a pidfd (natural for approaches
  like Binder\, where the allocator already knows the sender_pid) or a cgro
 upfd? What happens with buffers that may belong to different cgroups acros
 s their lifetime?
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2416/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2415@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T161000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T163000
SUMMARY:Page fault locking
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Kernel Memory Management MC · Matthew Wilcox · https://lpc.ev
 ents/event/20/contributions/2415/\n\nIn 2023 we introduced per-VMA locks t
 o solve contention and priority inversion on the mmap_lock for multithread
 ed programs.  While successful\, the initial implementation did not solve 
 every case of contention and some workloads can still demonstrate problems
 .  This session will discuss ways of fixing the remaining problems.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2415/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2420@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T173000
SUMMARY:Direct map fragmentation
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Kernel Memory Management MC · Kevin Brodsky · https://lpc.eve
 nts/event/20/contributions/2420/\n\nOn supported configurations\, the dire
 ct map is built using large\nPMD/PUD-level block mappings. This is expecte
 d to bring many of the\nbenefits of hugepages: improved TLB hit rate\, les
 s page table walking\,\nsmaller page table memory footprint.\n\nThis optim
 isation is most effective when the direct map gives access to\nthe entire 
 physical memory with uniform permissions. Unfortunately\, a\ngrowing numbe
 r of features need to remove pages from the direct map or\nmodify permissi
 ons/attributes at page granularity\, causing PMD/PUD\nblocks to be split d
 own to PTEs.\n\nAccording to results presented by Mike Rapoport at LSF/MM 
 2023 [1]\, the\nperformance impact of such fragmentation is negligible for
  data\naccesses. However\, recent measurements on x86 and arm64 suggest a 
 more\nnuanced picture\, and other factors such as power consumption should
  also\nbe considered.\n\nThis session will briefly summarise key results\,
  and then discuss\npossible ways to reduce fragmentation.\n\nQuestions for
  discussion:\n\n* Should direct map permissions be modelled as an allocati
 on property\n  in the buddy allocator\, or managed by higher-level interfa
 ces such as\n  execmem or secretmem?\n* Can x86 and arm64 use common logic
  for splitting and coalescing pages?\n\nA few interesting sources of direc
 t map fragmentation:\n\n* execmem (may use PMD-sized pools\; direct map PM
 Ds will still be split\n  if writing code)\n* secretmem\n* Confidential VM
 s including guest_memfd (encrypted/unmapped pages)\n* pkeys-based page tab
 le protection\n\nProposals to reduce fragmentation:\n\n* 2021/01 - PMD-siz
 ed pools for secretmem [2]\n* 2021/04 - Grouped vmalloc [3]\n* 2023/03 - `
 __GFP_UNMAPPED` based on dedicated cache [4]\n* 2026/02 - pkeys-protected 
 page table pools [5]\n* 2026/03 - `__GFP_UNMAPPED` based on migratype/page
 block [6]\n* 2026/06 - Collapsing blocks in secretmem [7]\n* 2026/06 - EXE
 CMEM_ROX_CACHE for arm64 with direct map PMD coalescing [8]\n\n[1] https:/
 /lwn.net/Articles/931406/\n[2] https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/20210121122723
 .3446-8-rppt@kernel.org/\n[3] https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/20210405203711.
 1095940-1-rick.p.edgecombe@intel.com/\n[4] https://lore.kernel.org/all/202
 30308094106.227365-1-rppt@kernel.org/\n[5] https://lore.kernel.org/linux-h
 ardening/20260227175518.3728055-1-kevin.brodsky@arm.com/\n[6] https://lore
 .kernel.org/lkml/20260320-page_alloc-unmapped-v2-0-28bf1bd54f41@google.com
 /\n[7] https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260603104624.36390-1-lance.yang@linux
 .dev/\n[8] https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260611130144.1385343-1-abarnas@go
 ogle.com/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2420/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2417@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T173000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T180000
SUMMARY:Accelerating Page Migration and Making the Migration Core Composabl
 e
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Kernel Memory Management MC · Shivank Garg\, Zi Yan · https:/
 /lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2417/\n\nAs the memory hierarchy deepen
 s at both ends\, with HBM adding a faster tier on top and CXL adding cheap
 er\, slower capacity below\, keeping hot data in the fast tier and sheddin
 g cold data downward makes page migration central to NUMA\, tiered-memory 
 and coherent CPU-GPU systems (where device memory is exposed as NUMA nodes
 ).\n\nProfiling move_pages(2) on EPYC Zen 6 shows that the folio copy domi
 nates migration (~96% of time for a 2MB THP)\, making it the primary scali
 ng bottleneck. Yet this path is largely sequential: folios are copied one 
 at a time by a single CPU\, while DMA engines\, idle cores and memory band
 width go unused.\n\nTo tap that idle hardware\, this work separates the fo
 lio content copy from the rest of migration and hands it to a pluggable mi
 grator. The batch path:\n - unmaps a batch of folios and flushes the TLB o
 nce\,\n - asks a migrator to copy the eligible folios\,\n - marks copied f
 olios so the move phase skips the per-folio copy\,\n - completes the move 
 through the existing flow [1].\n\nA faster copy is not the whole story: lo
 wer rmap overheads\, a simpler migration architecture and a composable cor
 e matter just as much. The work therefore proceeds in three directions: ac
 celerating the dominant copy phase\, batching the rmap walks and restructu
 ring the migration core so that these and other independent optimizations 
 compose instead of becoming special cases.\n\nAccelerating the copy\n-----
 ----------------\n\nThree complementary\, measured directions:\n1. dcbm - 
 a DMA-based migrator using dmaengine devices\, for bulk copy across multip
 le channels.\n2. mtcopy - multi-threaded CPU copy on idle cores\, a softwa
 re fallback where no DMA/offload engine is available [9].\n3. Bulk folio_c
 opy() for large folios: one copy over the contiguous range\, independent o
 f the batch and offload framework [2].\n\nMeasured with move_pages() on 1G
 B anonymous memory\, DRAM -> DRAM\, batch copy offload makes 2MB THP migra
 tion several times faster: ~6x with PTDMA (Zen 3\, 16 channels)\, ~3.5X wi
 th SDXI (Zen 6\, 1 channel) and ~3.8x with multi-threaded CPU copy (Zen 3\
 , mtcopy\, 8 threads).\n\nSeparately\, a related demotion effort uses non-
 temporal stores to reduce cache pollution and CXL-side read traffic [3].\n
 \nBeyond the copy: the rmap phase\n-------------------------------\n\nOnce
  the copy is offloaded\, rmap work dominates for PTE-mapped large folios (
 mTHP) because the unmap and restore walks handle each PTE separately. Batc
 hing those walks is the next lever\, and its payoff grows with folio size:
  on Zen 3\, a 1MB folio reaches ~5.6x over vanilla with DMA offload and rm
 ap batching\, compared to ~2x with offload alone [7].\n\nReworking the cor
 e so these compose\n-------------------------------------\n\nSeveral effor
 ts optimize different migration phases\, and each one adds flags or specia
 l cases to the current monolithic path [1][3][4]. They also run into the s
 ame limitation: ->migrate_folio() receives only enum migrate_mode\, which 
 describes the blocking semantics but not the migration intent (for example
 \, promotion versus demotion). So\, new behavior ends up either extending 
 migrate_mode or going through a side channel. For example\, non-temporal d
 emotion added MIGRATE_ASYNC_NON_TEMPORAL_STORES\, while the DMA offload re
 cords FOLIO_CONTENT_COPIED in migrate_info.\n\nTwo changes are posted as R
 FC[8]:\n\n1. Pass a small context struct (migrate_control) through migrate
 _pages() into ->migrate_folio()\, carrying mode\, reason\, and room for fu
 ture attributes. This separates blocking behavior from intent\, so the cop
 y phase knows why the folio is migrating and copy policy (cached\, non-tem
 poral\, offload\, and so on) can follow.\n\n2. Split the single list in mi
 grate_pages() into separate classes\, each with its own list and retries: 
 hugetlb folios\, movable_ops pages and LRU folios. This removes the type c
 hecks scattered through the shared batch path and makes the flow easier to
  follow.\n\nDiscussion\n----------\n\nI'd like to use the session to valid
 ate the direction of the migration core work and how ongoing efforts shoul
 d fit together.\n\nKey questions:\n\n- Interface: should migrate_pages() a
 nd ->migrate_folio() take a context struct? It touches every caller and ca
 llback. Is that one-time churn acceptable\, and is this the right shape?\n
 - Policy: Should each policy\, such as cache intent\, be stated explicitly
  by the caller\, or should the core derive it from the migration reason?\n
 - Core structure: is the per-class migration model right shape for maintai
 nability? movable_ops pages still use page->lru\, new_folio_t\, put_folio_
 t. What should a non-folio migration interface look like?\n- Synchronous f
 allback: after the async pass\, should the remaining folios be retried thr
 ough a direct single-folio path\, or should they keep reusing migrate_page
 s_batch() and avoid a second single-folio path?\n- Copy helpers: there is 
 no range version of copy_page() for bulk folio copy. memcpy() works\, but 
 without FSRM on x86 it falls back to an unrolled movq loop instead of copy
 _page()'s rep movsq. Should we add copy_pages()\, like clear_pages()?\n- E
 ngine selection: who should select the copy engine: the caller\, the provi
 der (as in the current RFC)\, or the core\, based on caller constraints an
 d provider capability? Should folio size\, batch size or topology drive th
 at choice?\n- Batching trade-offs: What is an acceptable trade-off between
  throughput and first-folio latency? Should the batch limit be a tunable p
 er platform?\n\nRoadmap:\n\nMigration core redesign and context passing\; 
 migration-side rmap batching\, bulk copy within large folios\, batch copy 
 and DMA offload\, Scatter-gather copy (DMA_MEMCPY_SG)\; SDXI migrator [6] 
 and DCBM driver\; topology-aware thread placement for multi-threaded copy 
 and CPU accounting for multi-threaded copy\; hot page promotion (pghot [5]
 ) reusing the offload path.\n\nReferences\n-----------\n[1] Migration batc
 h/offload\, RFC V6: https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260630-shivank-batch-mig
 rate-offload-v6-0-da95d7e8b8a2@amd.com\n[2] Bulk folio_copy optimization\,
  RFC v1: https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260427142036.111940-4-shivankg@amd.
 com\n[3] Non-temporal demotion\, RFC V2: https://lore.kernel.org/linux-mm/
 20260730-rfc-nt-demote-v2-0-452dbe3b5073@zptcorp.com/#t\n[4] migrate_mode/
 migrate_folio() ABI extensibility (Memory Hotness and Promotion call):\n  
   https://lore.kernel.org/linux-mm/37010218-ecad-4f75-961d-686d51b6677d@am
 d.com\n[5] pghot\, V8: https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260728054356.291998-1
 -bharata@amd.com\n[6] SDXI\, V3: https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260605-sdxi
 -base-v3-0-4d38ca2bdffe@amd.com\n[7] Migration-side rmap batching\, RFC V2
 : https://lore.kernel.org/linux-mm/20260813-migrate-rmap-batch-v2-0-3c5424
 c555c7@amd.com\n[8] Migration redesign and policy\, RFC V1: https://lore.k
 ernel.org/linux-mm/20260902-migrate-refactor-shivank-v1-0-9dcca87669c4@amd
 .com\n[9] mtcopy RFC v3: https://lore.kernel.org/all/20250923174752.35701-
 7-shivankg@amd.com
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2417/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2418@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T180000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T183000
SUMMARY:Tackling Isolation Issues Due to Memory Pressure
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Kernel Memory Management MC · Shakeel Butt · https://lpc.even
 ts/event/20/contributions/2418/\n\nOn a densely shared host\, a routine ev
 ent breaks tenant isolation: a task holding a shared kernel lock (filesyst
 em metadata\, a control-plane mutex/rwsem) allocates *inside* the critical
  section\, the allocation falls into memory reclaim\, and until reclaim fi
 nishes every other task waiting on that lock — from any cgroup — pays 
 the stall. Shared kernel locks aren't mediated by cgroups or memcg limits\
 , so this is cross-tenant priority inversion\, and overcommit makes it wor
 se.\n\nIn this talk we will present the data we have gathered across the M
 eta fleet on this problem\, and share how we think the solution should tak
 e shape. Rather than a finished design or patch series\, the goal is to la
 y out the problem space\, the constraints any fix must satisfy\, our curre
 nt direction\, and the open questions and to get early feedback from the u
 pstream community.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2418/
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
